course: dictionary
lesson: 8646
title: Sealant: keeping things out
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A sealant is a substance you put on a surface, or into a gap, to stop air, water or dirt getting through. Context gives clues: «Sealants and fluoride varnish are quick, painless coatings that help protect the back teeth from decay.» Coatings and protect tell you a sealant covers something to keep it safe.
explain: Look at the phrase «bathroom sealant». Around a bath or sink, sealant fills the gap where the wall meets the bath, so water does not leak out. Teeth work the same way: a sealant fills the grooves so food and germs cannot get in.
explain: Test your meaning in the sentence: «Fissure sealants are thin plastic coatings painted into the grooves of back teeth to keep food and bacteria out.» If sealant means a thin coating that keeps things out, the sentence makes sense.
fact: Sealant is a noun: one sealant, a sealant, or several sealants.
fact: A sealant stops air, water or dirt getting through.
fact: A dental sealant is a thin coating a dentist paints on the back teeth to protect them from decay.
fact: «Tooth sealants» help stop cavities in children's teeth.
fact: Sealants do not replace brushing, so you still need to brush your teeth.
exercise: Why is «bathroom sealant» put around a bath? | So the bath changes colour | So the bath can be moved to another room | *So water does not leak through the gap
exercise: Where does a dentist paint a dental sealant? | On the front teeth only | On the tongue | *On the back teeth
exercise: Find and copy one word from «Sealants and fluoride varnish are quick, painless coatings that help protect the back teeth from decay.» that means a layer covering something. | *coatings | quick | decay | painless
test: What does sealant mean? | A type of loud music | *A substance put on a surface or into a gap to stop air, water or dirt getting through | A small animal that lives in water | A tool for cutting metal pipes
test: Why are fissure sealants painted into the grooves of back teeth? | *To keep food and bacteria out | To stop the teeth from ever needing a brush | To change the colour of the teeth forever | To make the teeth taller
exam: Which sentence uses sealant correctly? | I ate a sealant for my lunch today. | Our teacher is called Sealant and she lives in Leeds. | The sealant barked loudly at the postman. | *Dad applied bathroom sealant to stop water leaking round the bath.
